B3 = "Book 3, Semiconductors and Devices,"
CH = {}; S2 = {}
CH[44] = dict(
title='Chapter 44. Gallium Arsenide: The Handset Amplifier and the pHEMT',
open="Open almost any phone and the cellular power amplifier is not silicon. It is a few square millimeters of gallium arsenide heterojunction bipolar transistors, with a silicon controller beside it, and it has held that place against twenty years of CMOS progress. The reasons are a breakdown voltage that suits a lithium-ion battery, a gain that suits a two-stage module, and ruggedness into a hand-held antenna.",
where="Cellular power-amplifier modules in phones and modems, Wi-Fi front-end modules, low-noise amplifiers in GPS and satellite receivers, and the pHEMT amplifiers and switches in microwave links and test equipment.",
blocks=[
('P', "Book 3's " + q("GaAs, GaN, and InP Are Not One Transistor") + " explains the devices: the InGaP/GaAs heterojunction bipolar transistor, HBT, and the pseudomorphic high-electron-mobility transistor, pHEMT. This chapter designs with them. The handset amplifier comes first, because it is the largest market for gallium arsenide, and the pHEMT low-noise amplifier second, because it is where the material's low noise matters most."),
('H2', '44.1 Why the handset amplifier stayed in gallium arsenide'),
('P', "An InGaP/GaAs HBT for handset amplifiers has a cutoff frequency near 30 GHz, a collector-emitter breakdown BVCEO of about 15 to 20 V, and a collector-base breakdown BVCBO of 30 V or more. Against a battery that runs from about 3.0 to 4.4 V, that leaves room for the swing to grow several times under mismatch without breakdown, which a fine-line CMOS device lacks. The HBT's current density is high, so the output device is small, and its transconductance is high, so two stages give the 28 to 30 dB a module needs. The semi-insulating substrate keeps the passive losses low, and the process is mature and cheap per die. CMOS has won the Wi-Fi and Bluetooth amplifier, Chapter 42, but the cellular one, with its higher power, higher linearity, and harder mismatch requirements, has mostly stayed in GaAs."),
('H2', '44.2 Sizing the output stage'),
('P', "A cellular handset in power class 3 must deliver 23 dBm at the antenna. The duplexer, switch, and tuner between the amplifier and the antenna cost 2 to 4 dB, and the linear signal of LTE and 5G needs a few decibels of back-off from saturation, so the output stage is sized for about 31 dBm saturated. The load line and the area follow from the supply, the knee, and a current density the emitter can carry for years."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 44.1', "Problem. Size a 1.95 GHz HBT output stage for 31 dBm saturated from 3.4 V with a 0.3 V knee in class B. Find Ropt, the peak collector current, and the emitter area at 0.2 mA/µm². Design a two-section match to 50 Ω and give each section's Q.",
 "Solution. 31 dBm is 1.26 W. Ropt = 3.1²/(2 × 1.26) = 3.82 Ω. The fundamental current is 2 × 1.26/3.1 = 0.812 A and the class B peak twice that, 1.62 A, which needs 1.62/(0.2 × 10⁻³) = 8,100 µm² of emitter, for example 100 fingers of about 81 µm². A single L-section from 3.82 Ω to 50 Ω would need Q = √(50/3.82 − 1) = 3.48. Two sections through the geometric mean, √(3.82 × 50) = 13.8 Ω, need Q = √(13.8/3.82 − 1) = 1.62 each, wider in bandwidth and less lossy, which is why modules use two or three sections, often with the first one on the die or the laminate right at the collector."),
('H2', '44.3 Bias, ballast, and thermal runaway'),
('P', "A bipolar transistor's base-emitter voltage falls by about 1.4 to 2 mV for every kelvin at constant current, so a finger that runs hotter than its neighbors draws more current, heats further, and can take the whole current, a failure called current hogging. A ballast resistor in each emitter or base breaks the loop. A finger with thermal resistance Rth that dissipates IcVCE warms by RthIcVCE, which would raise its current as if its VBE had been increased by φRthIcVCE, with φ the VBE temperature coefficient. The emitter resistance and the junction's own small-signal resistance VT/Ic oppose that, so the finger is stable when the total exceeds the thermal term."),
('EQ', "Re + VT/Ic > φ Rth VCE    ⇒    Re > φ Rth VCE − VT/Ic"),
('WE', 'Worked Example 44.2', "Problem. The output stage of Worked Example 44.1 has 100 fingers, each with Rth = 2,000 K/W, φ = 1.4 mV/K, and VCE = 3.4 V. Find the emitter ballast needed at the peak current per finger, and the equivalent base ballast for a current gain of 80.",
 "Solution. Per finger the peak current is 1.62 A/100 = 16.2 mA, so VT/Ic = 0.0259/0.0162 = 1.59 Ω, and φRthVCE = 1.4×10⁻³ × 2,000 × 3.4 = 9.52 Ω. Re must exceed 9.52 − 1.59 = 7.9 Ω; at the class B DC current per finger, 1.62/π/100 = 5.2 mA, the requirement relaxes to 4.5 Ω. A designer would choose about 9 Ω. In the emitter that costs 16.2 mA × 9 Ω = 0.15 V of swing and some gain, so modules usually put the ballast in the base, where β × Re = 80 × 9 = 720 Ω per finger has the same effect with no loss of collector swing, at the cost of bias drop that the bias circuit must supply."),
('P', "The bias circuit is a current mirror: a diode-connected reference transistor on the same die, at the same temperature, sets the base voltage of the power stage so that the quiescent current tracks temperature and process. A silicon controller beside the GaAs die sets the reference current, switches power modes, and handles the mobile industry processor interface, MIPI RFFE, through which the transceiver commands the module."),
('H2', '44.4 Linearity, tracking, and harmonic terminations'),
('P', "Handset amplifiers run in class AB with the second harmonic shorted near the collector, a step toward class F that raises efficiency a few points. Linearity is specified as adjacent-channel leakage ratio, ACLR, with the error-vector magnitude of the modulated signal. Average-power tracking with a DC-DC converter lowers the collector supply at low power, and envelope tracking, Chapter 36, follows the envelope on high-bandwidth signals; both set the supply below the battery and so use the HBT's breakdown margin for efficiency rather than for headroom."),
('H2', '44.5 Ruggedness into a hand-held antenna'),
('P', "A hand on a phone detunes its antenna, and a module must survive a 10:1 VSWR at every phase at the highest battery voltage. The collector swing can grow by 1 + |Γ|, here 1 + 9/11 = 1.82 times, so with a 4.35 V battery and a 0.3 V knee the collector peaks near 4.35 + 1.82 × 4.05 = 11.7 V, well below a BVCEO of 15 to 20 V. Breakdown at high current is lower than the DC rating suggests, because avalanche and heating interact, so modules add voltage-clamping diode stacks at the collector and limit the drive when a mismatch is detected, Chapter 31."),
('H2', '44.6 The pHEMT: low-noise amplifiers and switches'),
('P', "The pHEMT carries its current in a two-dimensional electron gas with no base current and therefore no shot-noise source at the input, so its minimum noise figure is tenths of a decibel at a few gigahertz and its noise resistance is small. Enhancement-mode pHEMTs, which turn on at a positive gate voltage, run from a single supply, and they are the input devices of most GPS, satellite, and microwave-link low-noise amplifiers. Worked Example 28.3 designs with one at 2 GHz, and Chapter 48 reads its Touchstone file and presents its optimum noise source. pHEMTs once made every handset switch; RF SOI has taken most of that market, Chapter 46, but pHEMT switches and amplifiers remain in microwave and millimeter-wave modules, and pHEMT power amplifiers serve Wi-Fi 5 and 6 GHz front ends where their gain at high frequency helps."),
('P', "Bipolar version, in reverse. Because this chapter's amplifier is already bipolar, the comparison runs the other way: a CMOS version of Worked Example 44.1 would need a stack, Section 42.2, because no single fine-line device survives an 11.7 V peak, and it would need a separate high-voltage protection scheme where the HBT relies on its breakdown margin."),
('KEY', "Key idea. The GaAs HBT keeps the handset amplifier because its breakdown suits a battery with margin to spare under mismatch, its current density makes a small output stage, and ballasting tames its thermal runaway. Size it by the load line, ballast every finger, and qualify it at a 10:1 VSWR."),
],
bench="On a module evaluation board, measure the quiescent current against temperature from −30 °C to +85 °C before any RF test; a mirror that does not track will show up as a drifting gain and ACLR long before it shows up as a failure. Run the ruggedness test with a mismatch tuner at every phase in steps of 15° at the highest battery voltage and maximum drive, and measure the output power at 50 Ω after each step: a module that survives but loses a few tenths of a decibel has damaged fingers.",
job="Power-amplifier module engineers at front-end companies design and qualify the GaAs dies, laminates, and controllers that go into every phone. Their days alternate between harmonic-balance simulation, layout reviews of the module substrate, and bench work on ACLR, efficiency, and ruggedness across bands and temperatures. A customer's antenna, measured in a real phone, often drives the last month of a project. A master's degree with RF circuit work is common, and the field values people who are equally comfortable with a load-pull bench and a thermal simulation.",
practice=["Find Ropt for an HBT stage delivering 29 dBm saturated from 3.4 V with a 0.3 V knee.",
 "For the stage of Problem 1 in class B, find the peak collector current and the emitter area at 0.25 mA/µm².",
 "Find the emitter ballast for a finger with Rth = 2,500 K/W, φ = 1.4 mV/K, VCE = 3.4 V, and Ic = 10 mA.",
 "Find the collector peak voltage at a 10:1 VSWR with a 4.35 V battery and a 0.3 V knee, in the worst phase.",
 "Design a two-section match from 6.05 Ω to 50 Ω through the geometric mean. Find the intermediate resistance and the Q of each section.",
 "Explain in one or two sentences why a pHEMT, not an HBT, is the usual choice for a GPS low-noise amplifier."],
wyau=(["Ropt = (VCC − Vk)²/(2P); class B peak current 4P/(VCC − Vk).",
       "Ballast: Re > φRthVCE − VT/Ic; base ballast β·Re.",
       "Multi-section match through the geometric mean √(R1R2).",
       "Collector peak under mismatch: VCC + (1 + |Γ|)(VCC − Vk)."],
      ["Emitter area from peak current and allowed current density.",
       "Second-harmonic termination at the collector."]),
)
S2[44] = {
1: "Idea: Ropt = (VCC − Vk)²/(2P). 29 dBm is 0.794 W. Ropt = 3.1²/(2 × 0.794) = 9.61/1.589 = 6.05 Ω. Check: 2 dB less power than Worked Example 44.1 raises the load by 10^0.2 = 1.585, from 3.82 to 6.05 Ω. The usual mistake is to use the battery voltage without the knee.",
2: "Idea: the class B peak is twice the fundamental current, 2 × 2P/V1. I1 = 2 × 0.794/3.1 = 0.512 A, so Ipk = 1.02 A, and the area is 1.02/(0.25 × 10⁻³) = 4,100 µm². Check: Worked Example 44.1 needed 8,100 µm² at 0.2 mA/µm² for 1.58 times the power; 8,100/1.585 × 0.2/0.25 = 4,090 µm². The usual mistake is to size the area from the average current.",
3: "Idea: Re > φRthVCE − VT/Ic. φRthVCE = 1.4×10⁻³ × 2,500 × 3.4 = 11.9 Ω; VT/Ic = 0.0259/0.010 = 2.6 Ω. Re > 9.3 Ω. Check: a hotter finger (larger Rth) needs more ballast, here more than Worked Example 44.2's 7.9 Ω. The usual mistake is to drop the VT/Ic term, which over-ballasts and wastes swing.",
4: "Idea: the swing grows by 1 + |Γ| under mismatch. |Γ| = 9/11 = 0.818. The peak is 4.35 + 1.818 × 4.05 = 11.7 V. Check: the result matches Section 44.5 and stays below a BVCEO of 15 to 20 V. The usual mistake is to use the 3.4 V nominal supply when the specification says the highest battery voltage.",
5: "Idea: two equal-ratio L-sections through √(R1R2). √(6.05 × 50) = 17.4 Ω. Q = √(17.4/6.05 − 1) = 1.37 for each section. Check: one section would need √(50/6.05 − 1) = 2.70. The usual mistake is to pick an intermediate value by eye; the geometric mean minimizes the largest Q.",
6: "Idea: an HBT's base current carries shot noise at the input, which a pHEMT, with no base current and a two-dimensional channel, does not have, so the pHEMT reaches a lower NFmin and a smaller Rn at the GPS frequency. Check: Worked Example 28.3's pHEMT has NFmin 0.45 dB and Rn 2 Ω at 2 GHz. The usual mistake is to choose the device with the higher gain; at the antenna, the noise figure decides.",
}
CH[45] = dict(
title='Chapter 45. Gallium Nitride: Broadband, Doherty, and Space Amplifiers',
open="A gallium nitride transistor the size of a grain of rice delivers tens of watts at 3.5 GHz from a 48 V rail, and an amplifier built from a few of them drives a 5G sector, an airborne radar module, or a satellite downlink. The material's high breakdown and high electron velocity make that possible; its traps, its negative gate bias, and its heat make it demanding.",
where="5G and 4G base-station amplifiers, radar transmit-receive modules, satellite solid-state power amplifiers, electronic warfare and test amplifiers spanning octaves, and the fast power switches of Book 6.",
blocks=[
('P', "Book 3 explains why a GaN high-electron-mobility transistor, HEMT, holds a high voltage and a high current at once: a wide bandgap gives a breakdown field near 3.3 MV/cm, and a two-dimensional electron gas at the AlGaN/GaN interface carries a dense, fast channel. On silicon carbide substrates, which conduct heat well, the result is a power density of about 4 to 6 W per millimeter of gate periphery at 28 V and around 10 W/mm at 48 to 50 V in production devices; laboratory devices have exceeded 30 W/mm at much higher voltages. This chapter designs three amplifiers: a broadband class J stage, a Doherty for a 3.5 GHz base station, and a space amplifier."),
('H2', '45.1 The device and its load line'),
('P', "A GaN device's high supply raises its optimum load toward 50 Ω, Section 22.4, which shrinks the matching networks and widens their bandwidth. The gate periphery follows from the power and the power density; the output capacitance, about 0.3 to 0.5 pF per millimeter, grows with it and sets the bandwidth limit of Chapter 11. GaN HEMTs are depletion-mode devices: they conduct at zero gate voltage and pinch off at a negative one, typically −2 to −4 V, so the gate must be biased negative before the drain is powered."),
('EQ', "gate periphery = P / (power density),    Ropt = (VDD − Vk)²/(2P)"),
('H2', '45.2 A broadband class J stage'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 45.1', "Problem. Design a 25 W class J stage covering 1.8 to 3.4 GHz from 28 V with a 3 V knee. Find the periphery at 5 W/mm, Ropt, the fundamental and second-harmonic impedances at the class J point, and the continuous-mode range. Check that the second harmonic of the lowest frequency stays out of the band.",
 "Solution. Periphery 25/5 = 5 mm. Ropt = 25²/(2 × 25) = 12.5 Ω. At the class J point Z₁ = 12.5 + j12.5 Ω and Z₂ = −j(3π/8) × 12.5 = −j14.7 Ω; across the continuous mode Z₁ = 12.5(1 + jα) Ω and Z₂ = −j14.7α Ω for α between −1 and +1, all at 70.1 % ideal efficiency with the knee. The output network need only keep the fundamental inside this region and the second harmonic on the matching reactive arc over the band, which a stepped low-pass network achieves over close to an octave. The lowest second harmonic, 3.6 GHz, sits above the top of the band at 3.4 GHz, so the network can treat fundamental and harmonic separately; a band wider than an octave would put harmonics inside the band, which is the practical limit of the method."),
('H2', '45.3 A Doherty for a 3.5 GHz base station'),
('P', "A 5G sector at 3.5 GHz transmits OFDM with a peak-to-average ratio of about 8 dB after crest-factor reduction. An asymmetric Doherty, Sections 23.3 and 41.6, puts its second efficiency peak at that back-off when 20 log(1 + r) = 8 dB."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 45.2', "Problem. Design a Doherty for 20 W average with an 8 dB peak-to-average ratio on 48 V GaN with a 4 V knee. Find r, the peak power, the main amplifier's share and its load at peak, the inverter impedance, the common load, and the peaking amplifier's load at peak. Give the ideal efficiency at the average power.",
 "Solution. 1 + r = 10^(8/20) = 2.512, so r = 1.512. The peak is 20 × 10^0.8 = 126.2 W. The main amplifier delivers the fraction 1/(1 + r) at peak, 50.2 W, and with a 44 V swing its load at peak is Rm = 44²/(2 × 50.2) = 19.3 Ω, which is also the inverter's characteristic impedance ZT. The common load is Rm/(1 + r) = 7.67 Ω, and the peaking amplifier, carrying r times the main current at peak, sees 7.67 × (1 + r)/r = 12.7 Ω. At the average power the main amplifier saturates into ZT²/RL = 48.4 Ω, 2.51 times its peak load, and the ideal efficiency is 78.5 % × 44/48 = 72.0 %. Measured amplifiers of this kind reach 45 to 55 % with predistortion; the inverter's bandwidth, the peaking device's gradual turn-on, and the 7.67 Ω common load, transformed to 50 Ω by a further network, account for most of the difference."),
('FIG', 'b4s_doherty.png', "Figure 45.1 — Left: the asymmetric Doherty of Worked Example 45.2, with main and peaking GaN devices, a 19.3 Ω quarter-wave inverter, and a 7.67 Ω common node. Right: ideal drain efficiency against output back-off for this Doherty (r = 1.512, peaks at 0 and −8 dB) and for a class B stage with the same knee.",
 "Left a schematic of a Doherty amplifier, right a plot of efficiency versus back-off with two peaks for the Doherty and a falling curve for class B."),
('H2', '45.4 Traps, memory, and bias sequencing'),
('P', "Charge trapped in the buffer and at the surface of a GaN device after a high-voltage excursion reduces the channel current for microseconds to seconds afterward. The effect, called current collapse or drain lag, makes the device's gain depend on the recent history of the signal, and it appears as memory in the predistortion of Chapter 38 and as pulse-to-pulse droop in radar. Field plates and surface passivation reduce it; predistortion models with long memory terms and bias held during gaps in a burst handle what remains."),
('P', "Because the device conducts at zero gate voltage, the supply sequence matters. Power up the negative gate supply first, to pinch-off, then the drain, then raise the gate to its quiescent point; power down in the reverse order. A drain applied with the gate at zero passes a current limited only by the supply and the device, which is usually enough to destroy it."),
('H2', '45.5 Heat'),
('P', "GaN's power density concentrates heat. A 25 W class J stage at 65 % efficiency draws 38.5 W and dissipates 13.5 W in a few square millimeters. With 3.5 K/W from channel to case and the case at 85 °C, the channel runs at 85 + 13.5 × 3.5 = 132 °C, comfortably below the 225 °C maximum many data sheets give, and below the lower temperatures their lifetime curves recommend for decades of service. Silicon carbide substrates make that possible; GaN on silicon, cheaper, conducts heat less well and is used where the power density is lower. GaN on diamond, in development, lowers the thermal resistance further, Chapter 46."),
('H2', '45.6 Space and radar amplifiers'),
('P', "Satellites have carried traveling-wave tubes for decades, Chapter 32, and still do at high power and high frequency, where a tube's efficiency of 60 % and more is hard to beat. GaN solid-state power amplifiers, SSPAs, have taken over at moderate power and in phased arrays, where hundreds of small amplifiers replace one tube and fail gracefully. A space amplifier is designed with extra margin: the channel temperature derated well below the terrestrial limit, radiation tolerance verified, and the supply sequencing built into the hardware. Radar transmit-receive modules run GaN in pulsed class AB or C at duty cycles of a few to about 20 %, which lets each device deliver more peak power than it could continuously."),
('P', "Bipolar version. No bipolar technology competes with GaN at these powers and frequencies; LDMOS is the silicon alternative below about 3.5 GHz, with a similar load line at 28 to 50 V but lower power density and lower efficiency at the high end of its range."),
('KEY', "Key idea. GaN's high supply raises the load toward 50 Ω and makes broadband and Doherty amplifiers practical at tens of watts. Its traps, negative gate bias, and concentrated heat are the price: predistort for memory, sequence the supplies, and design the thermal path as carefully as the match."),
],
bench="Build the bias sequencer before the amplifier and test it alone: with the drain supply connected to a resistive dummy load, confirm that the drain cannot rise until the gate is below pinch-off and that it falls before the gate returns to zero on power-down, including when the gate supply fails first. Then measure the quiescent current drift over the first minutes after power-up; a drift of a few percent is trapping and self-heating settling, and the bias loop or the predistortion must allow for it.",
job="Base-station and radar amplifier engineers design GaN stages and Doherty combiners from device models and load-pull data, tune them on the bench, and work with the predistortion team until the transmitter meets its emission mask at the target efficiency. Defense and space work adds pulsed operation, environmental testing, and documentation measured in binders. Employers include infrastructure vendors, radar and satellite builders, and device makers' application groups; a bachelor's degree with strong RF coursework is a common entry, often with a master's later.",
practice=["Find the gate periphery for 40 W at 5 W/mm.",
 "Find Ropt and the class J second-harmonic reactance for a 48 V device with a 4 V knee delivering 50 W.",
 "Find the Doherty asymmetry r that places the second efficiency peak 7 dB below full power.",
 "A GaN stage dissipates 30 W with 2.5 K/W from channel to case and the case at 80 °C. Find the channel temperature.",
 "A 100 W SSPA combines 8 equal amplifiers through a combiner with 0.5 dB of loss. Find the power each amplifier must deliver.",
 "Explain in one or two sentences why the gate supply of a GaN amplifier must be applied before the drain supply."],
wyau=(["Ropt = (VDD − Vk)²/(2P); periphery = P/(W/mm).",
       "Class J: Z₁ = Ropt(1 + jα), Z₂ = −j(3π/8)αRopt.",
       "Doherty: back-off peak 20 log(1 + r); Rm = ZT, RL = Rm/(1 + r).",
       "Channel temperature = Tcase + Pdiss Rth."],
      ["Peaking-amplifier load at peak RL(1 + r)/r.",
       "Combiner loss shared over N amplifiers."]),
)
S2[45] = {
1: "Idea: periphery = power/power density. 40/5 = 8 mm. Check: Worked Example 45.1 needed 5 mm for 25 W. The usual mistake is to use the laboratory record power density, which no production device sustains.",
2: "Idea: Ropt = (VDD − Vk)²/(2P) and Z₂ = −j(3π/8)Ropt. Ropt = 44²/100 = 19.4 Ω. Z₂ = −j1.178 × 19.4 = −j22.8 Ω. Check: Worked Example 41.4's 28 V, 10 W device had 31.3 Ω and −j36.8 Ω; the ratio of the two Z₂ values equals the ratio of the loads. The usual mistake is to drop the knee.",
3: "Idea: 20 log(1 + r) = back-off. 1 + r = 10^(7/20) = 2.239, r = 1.24. Check: 20 log 2.239 = 7.00 dB. The usual mistake is to use 10 log, which gives r = 4.0.",
4: "Idea: Tch = Tcase + PdissRth. 80 + 30 × 2.5 = 155 °C. Check: below a 225 °C maximum by 70 K. The usual mistake is to compute from the output power instead of the dissipated power.",
5: "Idea: the combiner's loss raises the total each amplifier must supply. 100 × 10^0.05 = 112.2 W in total, so 14.0 W each. Check: 8 × 14.0 W = 112 W, and 0.5 dB less is 100 W. The usual mistake is to divide 100 by 8 and miss the target by 11 %.",
6: "Idea: a GaN HEMT is a depletion-mode device that conducts fully at zero gate voltage. If the drain rises first, the device passes a current limited only by the supply and destroys itself; the negative gate supply must hold it pinched off first. Check: Section 45.4. The usual mistake is to treat the gate supply as a bias adjustment rather than a safety interlock.",
}
CH[46] = dict(
title='Chapter 46. RF SOI, SiGe, InP, and the Materials Still in the Lab',
open="Between a phone's amplifiers and its antennas sit dozens of switches, each a stack of a dozen transistors on a thin film of silicon over glass-like oxide. At the other end of the spectrum, silicon-germanium radar chips see the road at 77 GHz and indium phosphide transistors have amplified at a terahertz. This chapter designs the switch, sets the limits of the fast technologies, and surveys what comes next.",
where="Antenna and band switches and antenna tuners in phones, automotive and industrial radar chips, optical-link and instrument front ends, sub-terahertz research systems, and the tunable components of reconfigurable radios.",
blocks=[
('P', "Chapter 19 introduced RF switches and their figures of merit; Chapter 42 used stacks to raise a CMOS amplifier's voltage. Silicon on insulator, SOI, makes stacks practical, and it now builds nearly every switch and tuner in a phone. SiGe heterojunction bipolar transistors and InP devices extend silicon and compound technologies to millimeter and sub-millimeter waves. The figure of merit for each is different, and the chapter keeps them apart."),
('H2', '46.1 The RF SOI switch'),
('P', "In RF SOI the transistors sit in a thin silicon film over a buried oxide on a high-resistivity, trap-rich substrate. With no junction to the substrate, a transistor's body floats or is tied through a resistor, so a stack of many devices shares the RF voltage without any one junction breaking down, and the substrate adds little loss or nonlinearity. A switch arm is a series stack, turned on with a positive gate voltage and off with a negative one. Its figure of merit is the product Ron·Coff of one device, which does not depend on width: wider devices lower Ron and raise Coff in proportion. Leading processes reach about 80 to 90 fs."),
('EQ', "Vpk = √(2 P Z0),   N ≥ (1 + |Γ|) Vpk / Vdevice,   IL ≈ 20 log(1 + Ron/(2Z0)),   Coff = (Ron·Coff)/Ron"),
('WE', 'Worked Example 46.1', "Problem. Size a series switch arm for 35 dBm in a 50 Ω system with a 3:1 VSWR margin, each device holding 2.5 V. With a total on-resistance of 1.5 Ω and Ron·Coff = 100 fs, find the insertion loss, the off capacitance, and the isolation of the series arm alone at 2 GHz.",
 "Solution. 35 dBm is 3.16 W, so Vpk = √(2 × 3.16 × 50) = 17.8 V. At 3:1, |Γ| = 0.5 and the voltage can reach 1.5 × 17.8 = 26.7 V, so N ≥ 26.7/2.5 = 10.7, and 11 devices are stacked. The loss is 20 log(1 + 1.5/100) = 0.13 dB. The off capacitance is 100 fs/1.5 Ω = 66.7 fF, a reactance of 1,190 Ω at 2 GHz, and the isolation of the series arm alone is 20 log(|100 − j1,190|/100) = 21.6 dB. That is too little for most specifications, which is why practical switches add a shunt arm to ground on the off path, the series-shunt topology of Chapter 19, raising the isolation to 35 to 45 dB."),
('P', "An antenna tuner is the same technology used as a switched capacitor or inductor bank across the antenna's feed or aperture. A small, high-Q antenna stores energy, so the voltage across the tuning capacitors can exceed the transmitted signal's voltage several times, and the stack height is set by that voltage. Tuners let one compact antenna cover bands from 600 MHz to 6 GHz in turn."),
('H2', '46.2 SiGe: radar and millimeter waves'),
('P', "SiGe HBTs grade germanium into the base to accelerate electrons, reaching fT around 300 GHz and fmax around 500 GHz in the fastest production BiCMOS processes, with the CMOS logic of a radar chip on the same die. The price is a low breakdown, BVCEO about 1.5 V for the fastest devices, so SiGe amplifiers deliver tens of milliwatts, not watts. A transistor's maximum available gain falls about 6 dB per octave toward fmax, roughly 20 log(fmax/f), so a 500 GHz fmax gives about 16 dB at 77 GHz, enough for two or three stages of a radar receiver and the voltage-controlled oscillators of Chapter 49."),
('H2', '46.3 InP: toward a terahertz'),
('P', "Indium phosphide HEMTs and double-heterojunction bipolar transistors have the highest electron velocities of the mature technologies. InP HEMTs give the lowest noise figures at millimeter waves, which places them in radio-astronomy receivers, and in 2014 a ten-stage InP HEMT amplifier gained 9 dB at 1.0 THz, a record. InP DHBTs drive the fastest optical-link electronics. The wafers are small and expensive, so InP is used where nothing else performs."),
('H2', '46.4 The figure of merit across materials'),
('P', "The Johnson figure of merit, the product of breakdown field and saturated velocity divided by 2π, bounds the product of a transistor's breakdown voltage and its cutoff frequency. With representative values it is about 480 GHz·V for silicon, 640 GHz·V for gallium arsenide, and 7.9 THz·V for gallium nitride, which is why GaN holds tens of volts at frequencies where GaAs holds a few. It is a bound, not a design rule: SiGe's fast devices sit close to silicon's limit, and real GaN devices reach a fraction of theirs."),
('EQ', "JFOM = Ec vsat / (2π)  ≥  BV × fT"),
('FIG', 'b4s_techmap.png', "Figure 46.1 — An approximate map of where each technology delivers its power: saturated output per device against frequency for LDMOS, GaN on SiC, GaAs HBT and pHEMT, CMOS and RF SOI, SiGe, and InP. Regions are representative of 2026 production and research devices, not limits; they overlap, and the boundaries move every year.",
 "Log-log chart of output power versus frequency with overlapping shaded regions for each semiconductor technology."),
('H2', '46.5 Tunable components: BST and MEMS'),
('P', "Two technologies make reconfigurable filters and matching networks. Barium strontium titanate, BST, is a ferroelectric whose permittivity falls with applied field, so a thin-film capacitor tunes by about 2 to 3 to 1 with tens of volts, continuously but with some loss and nonlinearity. Microelectromechanical, MEMS, switches and capacitors move a metal beam electrostatically: they have almost no loss or distortion, but need tens of volts, switch in microseconds, and must survive billions of cycles. An electrostatic actuator is stable only until the gap closes to two-thirds of its rest value; beyond that, the beam snaps down, at the pull-in voltage."),
('EQ', "Vpull-in = √(8 k g0³ / (27 ε0 A))"),
('WE', 'Worked Example 46.2', "Problem. A MEMS switch has a spring constant of 10 N/m, a 2 µm gap, and a 100 µm × 100 µm electrode. Find its pull-in voltage.",
 "Solution. Vpi = √(8 × 10 × (2×10⁻⁶)³/(27 × 8.854×10⁻¹² × 10⁻⁸)) = √(6.4×10⁻¹⁶/2.39×10⁻¹⁸) = √268 = 16.4 V. A charge pump on the controller supplies it. The cube of the gap dominates: halving the gap would lower the voltage by 2.8 times, but would also lower the off-state isolation and the power the switch can hold before the RF voltage itself pulls the beam down."),
('H2', '46.6 Materials still in the laboratory'),
('P', "Gallium oxide, Ga2O3, has a bandgap near 4.8 eV and a projected breakdown field near 8 MV/cm, attractive for power switching, but its thermal conductivity is poor. Diamond has the highest thermal conductivity of any material and a wide bandgap; diamond transistors are research devices, while diamond heat spreaders under GaN, GaN on diamond, already lower channel temperatures in demonstrations. Aluminum nitride and high-aluminum AlGaN channels push the breakdown field higher still. Graphene, carbon nanotubes, and other two-dimensional materials have shown high carrier mobilities and cutoff frequencies in research, but no power gain at the frequencies that matter and no manufacturing path yet. For a designer, these are reasons to watch the literature, not to change a design."),
('P', "Bipolar version. Among these technologies the bipolar ones are SiGe and InP DHBTs; their designs follow the HBT rules of Chapter 44, ballast and all, at much lower voltages."),
('KEY', "Key idea. Each technology has its own figure of merit: Ron·Coff and stack voltage for SOI switches, fmax and breakdown for SiGe and InP, the Johnson limit for power at frequency. Choose the technology by the figure that matters for the block, and check it against the voltage the block will see under mismatch."),
],
bench="When characterizing a stacked switch, measure the second and third harmonics at the highest specified power and at the worst VSWR, not only the insertion loss. A stack whose voltage division is uneven, because of a gate resistor or a body-tie fault on one device, still passes small signals with normal loss but distorts or breaks down long before its rated power.",
job="Front-end module integration engineers combine power amplifiers, SOI switches and tuners, acoustic filters, and low-noise amplifiers from several processes into one laminate module for a phone. They own the module-level specifications, the band combinations, and the interactions: a switch harmonic that lands in a receive band, a filter that detunes when a tuner moves. Their tools are system budgets, circuit and electromagnetic simulation, and long test campaigns. Most hold a master's degree in RF engineering and grew into the role from one of the component specialties.",
practice=["Find the number of stacked devices for a switch handling 38 dBm in 50 Ω at a 4:1 VSWR, with 2.5 V per device.",
 "Find the insertion loss of a series switch with a total on-resistance of 2 Ω in a 50 Ω system.",
 "A switch has Ron = 1.5 Ω and Ron·Coff = 120 fs. Find Coff and the isolation of the series arm alone at 3 GHz.",
 "Estimate the maximum available gain at 140 GHz of a SiGe device with fmax = 500 GHz.",
 "Find the pull-in voltage of a MEMS switch with k = 20 N/m, a 3 µm gap, and a 100 µm × 100 µm electrode.",
 "Explain in one or two sentences why SOI, rather than bulk CMOS, builds switch stacks of ten or more devices."],
wyau=(["Vpk = √(2PZ0); stack N ≥ (1 + |Γ|)Vpk/Vdevice.",
       "Series switch loss 20 log(1 + Ron/(2Z0)); Coff = (Ron·Coff)/Ron.",
       "MAG ≈ 20 log(fmax/f) as a first estimate.",
       "MEMS pull-in √(8kg0³/(27ε0A))."],
      ["Johnson figure of merit Ec vsat/(2π).",
       "BST tuning ratio and MEMS gap scaling."]),
)
S2[46] = {
1: "Idea: Vpk = √(2PZ0), increased by 1 + |Γ|, divided by the device voltage. 38 dBm is 6.31 W, Vpk = 25.1 V. At 4:1, |Γ| = 0.6, so 1.6 × 25.1 = 40.2 V, and N ≥ 40.2/2.5 = 16.1, so 17 devices. Check: 3 dB more power and a worse VSWR than Worked Example 46.1 raise the count from 11 to 17. The usual mistake is to round 16.1 down.",
2: "Idea: a series resistance R in a matched line loses 20 log(1 + R/(2Z0)). 20 log(1.02) = 0.17 dB. Check: 1.5 Ω gave 0.13 dB; the loss is close to proportional for small R. The usual mistake is to use R/Z0 and double the loss.",
3: "Idea: Coff = 120 fs/1.5 Ω and the series isolation is 20 log(|2Z0 + Z|/(2Z0)). Coff = 80 fF, X = 1/(2π × 3×10⁹ × 80×10⁻¹⁵) = 663 Ω, and the isolation is 20 log(√(100² + 663²)/100) = 16.5 dB. Check: higher frequency and a worse figure of merit both lower the isolation from Worked Example 46.1's 21.6 dB. The usual mistake is to forget the shunt arm a real switch adds.",
4: "Idea: MAG falls about 20 dB per decade toward fmax. 20 log(500/140) = 11.1 dB. Check: at 77 GHz the same device gave 16.2 dB; 140 GHz is 5.2 dB lower, the ratio of frequencies in decibels. The usual mistake is to use fT in place of fmax for power gain.",
5: "Idea: Vpi = √(8kg0³/(27ε0A)), so Vpi scales as √(k g0³). From 16.4 V at 10 N/m and 2 µm: 16.4 × √(2 × 3.375) = 42.6 V; directly, √(8 × 20 × 27×10⁻¹⁸/(27 × 8.854×10⁻¹² × 10⁻⁸)) = 42.5 V. Check: the two agree within rounding. The usual mistake is to scale with the gap squared rather than cubed.",
6: "Idea: in bulk CMOS every stacked device's source and drain form junctions with the shared substrate, so the upper devices' junctions see the full stack voltage and break down. SOI isolates each device on oxide, so each sees only its share and the stack height is limited by the voltage per device. Check: Section 46.1. The usual mistake is to blame gate oxide breakdown, which limits each device, not the stack.",
}
CH[47] = dict(
title='Chapter 47. Acoustic and Micro-Mechanical Filters: SAW, BAW, XBAR, and the Smartphone Front End',
open="A 5G phone holds dozens of filters, and almost every one is a piece of vibrating crystal. Sound in a solid travels about a hundred thousand times slower than light, so an acoustic resonator at 2.5 GHz is a few micrometers across, has a Q near a thousand or more, and costs a few cents. Without it, a phone that talks on many bands at once could not exist.",
where="Duplexers and multiplexers in every phone, Wi-Fi coexistence filters, GPS and satellite receivers, the 433.92 MHz resonator in a garage-door remote, and the MEMS oscillators that keep time in watches, cars, and servers.",
blocks=[
('P', "Book 3's " + q("Piezoelectric Films and Acoustic Resonators") + " derives the acoustic resonator from its material: the Butterworth-Van Dyke model, the coupling coefficient kt², the SAW finger period, temperature drift, and the ladder that turns resonators into a band filter. This chapter designs with them, from a single resonator to a band filter, a duplexer, and the front-end module, and then surveys the acoustic and micromechanical devices beyond the filter."),
('H2', '47.1 The resonator as a design element'),
('P', "The model of Book 3 has a motional branch Lm, Cm, Rm in parallel with the plate capacitance C0. A designer sets C0 for the impedance level, the frequency fs by the film thickness or the finger period, and accepts the coupling kt² and the Q the technology gives. The spacing between series and parallel resonance, (fp − fs)/fp ≈ (4/π²) kt², bounds the widest band filter a technology can build."),
('EQ', "(fp − fs)/fp ≈ (4/π²) kt²,   Cm = C0 [(fp/fs)² − 1],   Lm = 1/(ωs² Cm),   Rm = ωs Lm / Q"),
('WE', 'Worked Example 47.1', "Problem. A BAW resonator at 3.5 GHz has kt² = 7 %, C0 = 1 pF, and Q = 1000. Find fp, the motional elements, and the fractional spacing, and decide whether a ladder of such resonators can pass the 5G band n78, 3.3 to 3.8 GHz.",
 "Solution. (fp − fs)/fp = (4/π²) × 0.07 = 2.84 %, so fp = 3.5/(1 − 0.0284) = 3.602 GHz. Cm = 1 pF × (1.0292² − 1) = 59.2 fF, Lm = 1/((2π × 3.5×10⁹)² × 59.2×10⁻¹⁵) = 34.9 nH, and Rm = 2π × 3.5×10⁹ × 34.9×10⁻⁹/1000 = 0.77 Ω. n78 is 500 MHz wide, 14 % of its center, five times the resonator's spacing, so no ladder of these resonators can pass it. Wide 5G bands use XBAR resonators in thin lithium niobate, whose coupling of roughly 25 % or more gives a spacing near 10 %, or lumped-element filters in glass and ceramic, or a split of the band."),
('FIG', 'b4s_bvd.png', "Figure 47.1 — Above: impedance magnitude of the 3.5 GHz BAW resonator of Worked Example 47.1, minimum at fs = 3.50 GHz and maximum at fp = 3.60 GHz. Below: transmission of the seven-resonator Band 7 transmit ladder of Worked Example 47.2 (BVD model, kt² = 9 %, Q = 1500), with the transmit band 2500 to 2570 MHz and the receive band 2620 to 2690 MHz marked.",
 "Two plots: resonator impedance in ohms versus frequency with a dip and a peak, and a ladder filter transmission versus frequency with a flat passband and steep skirts."),
('H2', '47.2 From resonators to a band filter'),
('P', "A ladder places series resonators in the signal path and shunt resonators to ground, with the shunt resonators lowered in frequency so that their parallel resonance falls on the series resonators' series resonance. The passband sits between the shunt series resonance, which shorts the path below the band, and the series parallel resonance, which opens it above. The steepest skirt can be placed on whichever side matters by choosing the resonator frequencies; a transmit filter puts it on the side toward the receive band."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 47.2', "Problem. Design a Band 7 transmit filter, 2500 to 2570 MHz, which must reject the receive band, 2620 to 2690 MHz, by at least 50 dB, using AlScN BAW resonators with kt² = 9 % and Q = 1500 in a ladder of four series and three shunt resonators. Choose the resonator frequencies and plate capacitances and report the simulated loss, return loss, and rejection.",
 "Solution. The spacing is (4/π²) × 0.09 = 3.65 %. To put the upper skirt between the bands, the series resonators' parallel resonance must sit near 2.63 GHz, so their series resonance is about 2.534 GHz; the shunt resonators are lowered by the spacing to about 2.434 GHz, putting their parallel resonance, 2.526 GHz, near the series fs. Plate capacitances of 0.81 pF in series and 1.74 pF in shunt set the impedance level near 50 Ω. A circuit simulation of the BVD ladder, Figure 47.1, gives 0.40 to 0.60 dB of loss across the transmit band, return loss better than 13 dB, and at least 50 dB of rejection across the receive band, 68 dB at its lower edge. The model omits electrode resistance, package parasitics, and coupling between input and output, which in a real part raise the loss toward 1 to 2 dB and limit the far rejection; the design is a starting point that an electromagnetic and acoustic simulation then refines."),
('H2', '47.3 Duplexers, multiplexers, and their budget'),
('P', "A duplexer joins a transmit and a receive filter at the antenna, each designed to look like an open circuit in the other's band. Two numbers set its isolation. The transmitter's own noise in the receive band must fall below the receiver's noise floor, and the transmitted signal must not overload the receiver's low-noise amplifier."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 47.3', "Problem. A handset amplifier produces +27 dBm in the transmit band and −135 dBm/Hz of noise in the receive band. The receiver's noise figure is 3 dB. Find the isolation the duplexer needs in the receive band to keep the transmitter's noise 10 dB below the receiver's floor, and the transmit-band power at the low-noise amplifier with 50 dB of isolation in the transmit band.",
 "Solution. The receiver's floor is −174 + 3 = −171 dBm/Hz, so the transmitter noise must reach the receiver at no more than −181 dBm/Hz, and the receive-band isolation must be at least −135 − (−181) = 46 dB. With 50 dB of transmit-band isolation, the transmitted signal reaches the low-noise amplifier at 27 − 50 = −23 dBm, which its compression point must absorb without blocking. A duplexer of about 50 dB in both bands therefore suits this phone; the 2620 MHz edge is the hard one, because the duplex gap of Band 7 is only 50 MHz, 1.9 % of the frequency."),
('P', "Carrier aggregation transmits or receives on several bands at once, so a phone's antenna port joins several filters in a multiplexer, each of which must look open in all the others' bands. Quadplexers and hexaplexers are built from acoustic filters with phase-shifting networks at the common node, and the front-end module packs them with switches, amplifiers, and tuners."),
('H2', '47.4 Temperature, guard bands, and technology choice'),
('P', "A filter's frequency drifts by its temperature coefficient of frequency, TCF, times the temperature range, and a phone works from about −30 °C to +85 °C, 115 K. With Book 3's values, a lithium tantalate SAW at −35 ppm/K moves 4,025 ppm, 10.3 MHz at 2.57 GHz, and an AlN BAW at −25 ppm/K moves 2,875 ppm, 7.4 MHz, each a large fraction of Band 7's 50 MHz gap once manufacturing spread is added. Temperature-compensated SAW, TC-SAW, which covers the fingers with silicon dioxide, cuts the drift to a third or less, and thin-film SAW on bonded substrates, sold under names such as I.H.P. SAW, raises Q and lowers drift together."),
('P', "The result is a working division of the spectrum. SAW and TC-SAW cover most bands below about 2 GHz and many up to 2.7 GHz at the lowest cost. BAW, in its free-standing FBAR and solidly mounted SMR forms, covers 1.5 to about 6 GHz and the hardest duplex gaps. XBAR and other laterally excited lithium niobate resonators cover the wide 5G bands n77, n78, and n79 and the 5 to 7 GHz Wi-Fi bands. Integrated passive devices in glass and LTCC cover very wide bands where acoustic coupling runs out. In 2022 Murata acquired Resonant, the developer of XBAR, for about $290 million, which signals how much these wide bands are worth."),
('FIG', 'b4s_fem.png', "Figure 47.2 — A simplified smartphone front end for one antenna: power amplifiers and low-noise amplifiers connect through acoustic duplexers and multiplexers to an RF SOI band switch, an antenna tuner, and the antenna. Each technology appears in its usual place; real modules repeat this chain for several antennas and band groups.",
 "Block diagram of a phone front-end module showing PA, LNA, duplexers, multiplexer, band switch, tuner and antenna."),
('H2', '47.5 Hobby filters and resonators'),
('P', "The cheapest acoustic devices are the ones a hobbyist meets first. Garage-door remotes, weather stations, and key fobs at 433.92 MHz use a one-port SAW resonator as the frequency reference of a one-transistor oscillator, and SAW filters clean up the receivers. On ST-cut quartz, with a velocity near 3,158 m/s, a 433.92 MHz resonator has an electrode period of 7.28 µm and fingers 1.82 µm wide, coarse by modern standards, which is why these parts cost cents. A NanoVNA, Chapter 52, measures their series and parallel resonances directly."),
('H2', '47.6 Beyond the filter'),
('B', "MEMS timing: silicon resonators replace quartz crystals in many oscillators, with electronic temperature compensation and much better shock resistance; they are now in cars, servers, and wearables."),
('B', "Thin-film lithium niobate, TFLN: the material of XBAR is also the platform of fast electro-optic modulators, Chapter 50, so acoustic, optical, and RF functions may meet on one chip."),
('B', "Reconfigurable filters: banks of fixed acoustic filters selected by SOI switches dominate today; tunable acoustic resonators, using ferroelectric films whose coupling changes with bias, are in research."),
('B', "Acoustic delay lines: a SAW delay line stores microseconds of signal in millimeters, once the basis of radar pulse compression and still used in sensors and identification tags."),
('B', "Acoustic and modulated circulators: modulating a resonator's properties in time and space breaks reciprocity without a magnet, a research path to integrated circulators, Chapter 15."),
('P', "The outlook is more bands, wider bands, higher frequencies, and more filters per phone, with XBAR and other lithium niobate structures growing fastest and BAW holding the hardest gaps."),
('KEY', "Key idea. An acoustic filter is a ladder of resonators whose coupling sets the widest band it can pass and whose Q and temperature drift set how close it can sit to its neighbor. Choose SAW, TC-SAW, BAW, or XBAR by band, width, and gap, and size the duplexer from the transmitter's noise and the receiver's floor."),
],
bench="Measure an acoustic filter on a VNA with the test fixture de-embedded, and then again with the board heated and cooled across the temperature range; a passband that looks comfortable at room temperature can slide into the duplex gap at +85 °C. Keep the drive level low when you measure the resonances, because a few hundred milliwatts can shift a small SAW resonator by heating, and record the level with the data.",
job="Acoustic filter design engineers turn band specifications into resonator layouts, ladders, and multiplexers, using finite-element acoustic simulation, circuit optimization, and wafer-level measurement. They work closely with process engineers on film thickness and trimming, and with module engineers on how the filter behaves in its package. Employers are the few large filter makers and a growing number of startups; the work suits people with a mix of physics and circuits, and many hold a PhD or a master's in electrical engineering or physics.",
practice=["A resonator at fs = 2.0 GHz has kt² = 6.5 %. Find fp and the resonance spacing in megahertz.",
 "Find the drift of a SAW filter with TCF = −35 ppm/K at 1.9 GHz over 115 K.",
 "Find the electrode period and finger width of a 433.92 MHz SAW resonator on ST-cut quartz at 3,158 m/s.",
 "A transmitter has −135 dBm/Hz of noise in the receive band and the receiver's noise figure is 3 dB. Find the isolation that keeps the noise 10 dB below the receiver's floor.",
 "Find Rm for the resonator of Worked Example 47.1 if its Q rises to 1500.",
 "Explain in one or two sentences why XBAR resonators, rather than AlN BAW, serve the band n78."],
wyau=(["(fp − fs)/fp ≈ (4/π²)kt²; Cm = C0[(fp/fs)² − 1], Lm = 1/(ωs²Cm), Rm = ωsLm/Q.",
       "SAW period = v/f, fingers a quarter of the period.",
       "Drift = TCF × ΔT × f.",
       "Duplexer isolation = transmitter noise − (receiver floor − margin)."],
      ["Ladder rule: shunt fp at the series fs.",
       "Technology map by band, width, and gap."]),
)
S2[47] = {
1: "Idea: (fp − fs)/fp = (4/π²)kt². The fraction is 2.63 %, so fp = 2.0/(1 − 0.0263) = 2.054 GHz, a spacing of 54 MHz. Check: Book 3's 2.45 GHz AlN example gave 66 MHz; scaling by 2.0/2.45 gives 54 MHz. The usual mistake is to use kt² itself as the spacing.",
2: "Idea: drift = TCF × ΔT × f. 35 × 115 = 4,025 ppm; 4,025×10⁻⁶ × 1.9 GHz = 7.6 MHz. Check: at 2.57 GHz the same part drifts 10.3 MHz, Section 47.4. The usual mistake is to use the 25 °C to 85 °C range only.",
3: "Idea: the period is one acoustic wavelength, v/f, and each finger is a quarter of it. 3,158/433.92×10⁶ = 7.28 µm, and the fingers are 1.82 µm. Check: the 2 GHz tantalate SAW of Book 3 had a 2.08 µm period; at a lower frequency and velocity the period scales as v/f. The usual mistake is to make the finger half a wavelength.",
4: "Idea: the transmitter's noise must reach the receiver 10 dB below −174 + NF. The floor is −171 dBm/Hz, the target −181 dBm/Hz, so the isolation is −135 + 181 = 46 dB. Check: the same as Worked Example 47.3. The usual mistake is to subtract the transmitter's carrier power instead of its noise density.",
5: "Idea: Rm = ωsLm/Q. Rm = 2π × 3.5×10⁹ × 34.9×10⁻⁹/1500 = 0.51 Ω. Check: 0.77 Ω × 1000/1500 = 0.51 Ω. The usual mistake is to change Lm with Q; Q changes only the loss.",
6: "Idea: n78 spans 14 % of its center, and an AlN BAW resonator's spacing is only about 2.8 %, far too narrow. XBAR resonators in thin lithium niobate have couplings of roughly 25 % or more, a spacing near 10 %, which with band splitting and design tricks covers these wide bands. Check: Worked Example 47.1. The usual mistake is to think a higher Q widens a filter; coupling sets the width, Q sets the loss.",
}
